WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.780 --> 00:00:03.347
A tidy answer can still be wrong.

2
00:00:03.367 --> 00:00:05.827
Our invented Wellington HR team met about

3
00:00:05.847 --> 00:00:07.987
a buddy programme for new staff.

4
00:00:08.007 --> 00:00:09.587
Imagine the summary says,

5
00:00:09.607 --> 00:00:11.127
approved.

6
00:00:11.257 --> 00:00:12.837
Now check the notes.

7
00:00:12.857 --> 00:00:14.437
It was discussed.

8
00:00:14.457 --> 00:00:15.877
Not approved.

9
00:00:15.897 --> 00:00:17.517
Before you share a summary,

10
00:00:17.537 --> 00:00:19.557
check what the source actually says,

11
00:00:19.577 --> 00:00:22.217
and keep the decision with a person.

12
00:00:23.797 --> 00:00:26.284
The Privacy Act twenty twenty still applies

13
00:00:26.304 --> 00:00:29.244
when you use personal information with AI.

14
00:00:29.264 --> 00:00:31.404
Enterprise data protection doesn't replace

15
00:00:31.424 --> 00:00:34.224
your organisation's responsibilities.

16
00:00:34.354 --> 00:00:36.521
The Privacy Commissioner recommends assessing

17
00:00:36.541 --> 00:00:38.961
the privacy impact before you start using

18
00:00:38.981 --> 00:00:40.121
an AI tool,

19
00:00:40.141 --> 00:00:43.581
and updating that assessment as things change.

20
00:00:44.561 --> 00:00:45.981
So, for practice,

21
00:00:46.001 --> 00:00:48.381
use invented or public material.

22
00:00:48.401 --> 00:00:50.261
Keep passwords, tokens,

23
00:00:50.281 --> 00:00:53.841
payroll, and sensitive staff records out.

24
00:00:53.971 --> 00:00:56.351
And removing a name isn't enough.

25
00:00:56.371 --> 00:00:58.191
An address, a job title,

26
00:00:58.211 --> 00:01:00.671
or a mix of details can still point to one

27
00:01:00.691 --> 00:01:01.791
person.

28
00:01:01.811 --> 00:01:04.471
If a real task needs personal information,

29
00:01:04.491 --> 00:01:07.091
talk to your privacy lead first.

30
00:01:08.071 --> 00:01:09.971
Before you share anything,

31
00:01:09.991 --> 00:01:11.651
check five things.

32
00:01:11.671 --> 00:01:12.771
The source.

33
00:01:12.791 --> 00:01:13.811
The facts.

34
00:01:13.831 --> 00:01:14.851
The meaning.

35
00:01:14.871 --> 00:01:15.971
The audience.

36
00:01:15.991 --> 00:01:16.891
And the owner,

37
00:01:16.911 --> 00:01:19.031
the person responsible.

38
00:01:20.361 --> 00:01:21.701
Open the reference.

39
00:01:21.721 --> 00:01:23.861
Confirm the numbers and the dates.

40
00:01:23.881 --> 00:01:26.501
A citation, or a confident sentence,

41
00:01:26.521 --> 00:01:27.941
isn't proof.

42
00:01:27.961 --> 00:01:29.861
New Zealand's public service guidance says

43
00:01:29.881 --> 00:01:33.061
the same: verify what AI generates against

44
00:01:33.081 --> 00:01:35.161
trusted sources.

45
00:01:36.141 --> 00:01:38.481
Next, a simple team rule.

46
00:01:38.501 --> 00:01:40.961
The lesson's starter names the approved account,

47
00:01:40.981 --> 00:01:42.161
the allowed tasks,

48
00:01:42.181 --> 00:01:44.641
the reviewer, and who to contact if something

49
00:01:44.661 --> 00:01:45.761
goes wrong.

50
00:01:45.781 --> 00:01:46.841
Mark it as a draft,

51
00:01:46.861 --> 00:01:48.701
and get it approved.

52
00:01:49.631 --> 00:01:52.011
Your team rule should also say what to do

53
00:01:52.031 --> 00:01:53.531
after a mistake.

54
00:01:53.551 --> 00:01:55.811
Name the contact and the reporting route,

55
00:01:55.831 --> 00:01:58.251
so anyone who shares the wrong thing knows

56
00:01:58.271 --> 00:01:59.951
the next step.

57
00:02:00.931 --> 00:02:02.191
Then practise.

58
00:02:02.211 --> 00:02:03.271
Five minutes a day,

59
00:02:03.291 --> 00:02:04.591
for the next week.

60
00:02:04.611 --> 00:02:06.751
One small task each day.

61
00:02:06.771 --> 00:02:07.751
Keep the source,

62
00:02:07.771 --> 00:02:09.271
the prompt, the revision,

63
00:02:09.291 --> 00:02:11.331
and a short check note.

64
00:02:11.461 --> 00:02:13.974
You'll soon see which checks come easily,

65
00:02:13.994 --> 00:02:15.601
and where you'd like some help.

66
00:02:15.621 --> 00:02:18.501
That's a good guide to what to learn next.

67
00:02:18.631 --> 00:02:20.958
And talk to a colleague or your manager about

68
00:02:20.978 --> 00:02:22.638
one useful habit.

69
00:02:22.658 --> 00:02:24.718
Agree a task your workplace allows,

70
00:02:24.738 --> 00:02:25.758
who reviews it,

71
00:02:25.778 --> 00:02:28.338
and where the data boundary sits.

72
00:02:29.318 --> 00:02:31.858
Finish the quiz and complete the lessons.

73
00:02:31.878 --> 00:02:33.858
Signed-in learners who meet the requirements

74
00:02:33.878 --> 00:02:36.758
can get TheColab's course certificate.

75
00:02:36.888 --> 00:02:37.908
When you're ready,

76
00:02:37.928 --> 00:02:40.508
there are more courses and workshops at copilot

77
00:02:40.528 --> 00:02:42.148
training dot co dot N Z,

78
00:02:42.168 --> 00:02:43.928
slash courses.

79
00:02:44.058 --> 00:02:45.538
Thanks for learning with us.

80
00:02:45.558 --> 00:02:48.098
This is independent training from The Colab.

81
00:02:48.118 --> 00:02:50.918
It's not affiliated with Microsoft.
